          "headline": "Post-training objective recovers 56.3% of chain-of-thought monitorability lost to compression while keeping 80.2% of the token savings",
          "sources": [
            { "name": "arXiv", "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29734" }
          ],
          "bullets": [
            "arXiv:2610.29734 proposes \"an unsupervised post-training objective based on maximizing mutual information between CoT and the output for improving CoT monitorability,\" and reports that \"Across three model organisms with degraded CoT, it recovers most or all of the lost monitorability.\" The PDF gives the headline figure: from the 200-step checkpoint on GPQA, post-training at one setting \"recovers 56.3% of the loss in mean monitorability and 60.8% of the loss in worst-case monitorability while preserving 80.2% of the original token savings.\"",
            "Out of distribution the recovery shrinks: on MMLU-Pro the same checkpoint \"recovers 42.4% of the loss in mean monitorability while preserving 82.2% of the original token savings,\" and on AIME \"it recovers 23.6% while preserving 72.5%.\"",
            "The degradation being repaired is small in absolute terms: average monitorability on GPQA-Diamond \"falls from 3.794 to 3.617\" after 100 steps and \"to 3.480 after 200 steps.\" Readers should weigh the percentages against that baseline rather than against zero.",
            "Authors are Usman Anwar (Cambridge, work done while at Qualcomm AI Research), Tim Bakker and Christos Louizos (Qualcomm AI Research) and David Krueger (Mila, Université de Montréal). A preprint, with the figures taken from the PDF rather than the abstract."
