        { "host": "B", "text": "The AWS Security Blog says Amazon Threat Intelligence found an exploitation attempt dating to October 17th in its honeypot telemetry, four days before public disclosure, from an address tied to a commercial anonymization network. Amazon says it remains unclear how the actor got exploit information before disclosure." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "What's the general lesson Amazon draws?" },
        { "host": "B", "text": "Amazon says AI-assisted analysis makes it cheaper to turn a patch diff or an advisory into a working exploit, so more actors can act within hours or even minutes once details are disclosed. Its advice is to measure patch timelines for internet-facing systems in hours." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "How much of that is confirmed?" },
        { "host": "B", "text": "These are the company's own numbers from its own honeypots, a single source, not independently verified, and no other vendor has corroborated the October 17th attempt. Amazon says it's unclear whether any of those attempts would have succeeded against real systems." }
      ]
