    {
      "type": "item",
      "section": "Security, misuse & threat intelligence",
      "headline": "PHP ships emergency patches for two 9.2-rated remote-code-execution flaws that frontier models proved exploitable after a decade",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "B", "text": "PHP shipped emergency patches yesterday for two unauthenticated remote code execution flaws, both rated 9.2. CISA says they affect all PHP installations serving web requests, and tells anyone who can't patch to take their PHP systems offline." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "How were they found?" },
        { "host": "B", "text": "Wiz Research says it used Wiz Atlas, Gemini 4 Argon and GPT-5.6 Sol throughout. Wiz says every PHP version released in the last decade is affected by at least one of them, going back to version 5.4." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "And what makes this an AI story rather than just a bad bug?" },
        { "host": "B", "text": "Wiz says demonstrating exploitability required an exceptionally complex chain of steps, at the edge of what human researchers could practically achieve, and that frontier AI models substantially reduced that barrier." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "The PHP Foundation's own account is unusually blunt about the delay." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "It is. A core developer found the first signs of the same root cause in 2024, and a fix stalled over compatibility and resourcing. The Foundation says the delay was a structural failure, caused by both the security team's decision process and its lack of resources." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "Who reported the working exploit, and how exposed is everybody?" },
        { "host": "B", "text": "The Foundation says Calif's researcher Ryan A reported an unconditional remote code execution on August 11th, and that OpenAI independently reported the same root cause the same day. Palo Alto Unit 42 says it found over 22.6 million exposed instances potentially vulnerable. Those are company claims, not independently verified, and Unit 42 says it had seen proof-of-concept code but no confirmed exploitation of real systems when it published." }
      ]
    },
